%N A164850 Numbers for which the fourth power of the sum of their digits equals the product of their digits.
%C A164850 For every positive integer x, there are infinitely many numbers for which (sum of digits)^x = (product of digits). For any sufficiently large k, consider the number consisting of 2^k - 2kx ones followed by kx twos. Its digit sum is 2^k and its digit product is 2^(kx) = (2^k)^x. In particular, taking x = 4 and k >= 6 shows that this sequence is infinite. - _Adam Reichert_, Aug 21 2026 [Correcting the previous comment by Boris Hostnik, Sep 04 2009.]
%e A164850 88888888 is in the sequence because (8+8+8+8+8+8+8+8)^4 = 16777216 = 8*8*8*8*8*8*8*8. [Corrected by _Adam Reichert_, Aug 21 2026]
